},{"pos":53184,"endPos":53230,"startLine":1281,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":1281,"endLineOffset":54,"length":47},{"pos":53257,"endPos":53270,"startLine":1282,"startLineOffset":26,"endLine":1282,"endLineOffset":39,"length":14},{"pos":53360,"endPos":53401,"startLine":1284,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":1284,"endLineOffset":49,"length":42}],"dstChanges":[{"pos":52659,"endPos":52704,"startLine":1265,"startLineOffset":7,"endLine":1265,"endLineOffset":52,"length":46},{"pos":52712,"endPos":52761,"startLine":1266,"startLineOffset":7,"endLine":1266,"endLineOffset":56,"length":50},{"pos":52802,"endPos":52875,"startLine":1268,"startLineOffset":11,"endLine":1268,"endLineOffset":84,"length":74},{"pos":53158,"endPos":53162,"startLine":1278,"startLineOffset":26,"endLine":1278,"endLineOffset":30,"length":5},{"pos":53189,"endPos":53231,"startLine":1279,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":1279,"endLineOffset":50,"length":43},{"pos":53303,"endPos":53348,"startLine":1281,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":1281,"endLineOffset":53,"length":46}]}]},{"comment":"The `MvExpand` constructor now includes a `limit` parameter, and this change is propagated through various parts of the codebase. However, there are several places where the `limit` value is being handled or checked without proper validation or consideration for null values. Specifically:\n- In `#XN4DE`, the `limit` is directly passed to the new `MvExpand` instance without any checks.\n- In `#89XMM`, when adjusting the `mvxLimit` in the `PushDownAndCombineLimits` rule, there are checks for null and negative values, but no check for whether `mvxLimit` exceeds `limitVal`. This could lead to unexpected behavior if `mvxLimit` is greater than `limitVal`.\n- In `#QV1PZ`, `#QJD2X`, and `#X7MHK`, the tests assume that `mvExpand.limit()` will always return a non-null value, which might not be the case if the `MvExpand` instance was created without an explicit limit.\nTo improve robustness:\n- Add null checks where `limit` is used.\n- Ensure that `mvxLi